# Gitleaks baseline for the CI `security` job (.github/workflows/ci.yml).
#
# Format: one FINGERPRINT per line -> commit:file:rule:line
#
# RULES:
#   1. Only HISTORICAL findings belong here - leaks that are already committed
#      and therefore cannot be removed without rewriting published history.
#   2. A NEW secret must never be added here. Rotate it and remove it instead.
#   3. Every entry states what the value was, whether it is considered
#      compromised, and what was done about it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 36ee5d513c1ca05d2a8896b1c2c7c1b9cccbc687:.env.example:generic-api-key:12
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT: a concrete 64-hex SECRET_KEY committed to `.env.example` on 2026-07-30.
#
# STATUS: treated as COMPROMISED. It sat in a public-facing example file, so it
# must be assumed known. It signs JWTs, so anything that ever ran with it could
# have had tokens forged against it.
#
# ACTION TAKEN (2026-08-27): the value was replaced in the working tree with an
# obviously-invalid placeholder plus the `openssl rand -hex 32` command that
# generates a real one. `.gitignore` now denies `.env.*` with only the two
# `*.example` templates re-allowed, so a real env file cannot follow it in.
#
# WHY IT IS STILL SUPPRESSED: the commit is already in history. Removing it
# requires a force-push that rewrites published history and breaks every clone -
# not something to do silently as part of a deploy change. The finding is
# acknowledged here instead.
#
# DECISION (2026-08-27, Adebayo): ROTATE, DO NOT PURGE. Production generates its
# own SECRET_KEY on the server (see .env.production.example), so the committed
# value is never the key any deployed environment runs with. Once it signs
# nothing, the copy left in history is a dead 64-hex string and clearing it buys
# no security - while a git-filter-repo force-push would rewrite published
# history and break every existing clone and open PR. The cost is real and the
# benefit is zero, so the finding stays acknowledged here.
#
# THIS DOES NOT EXPIRE: if some environment is later found to have run with this
# key, rotating that environment's SECRET_KEY is still mandatory (it logs out
# every user). The decision above is that history surgery is unnecessary, not
# that the value is safe to use.
36ee5d513c1ca05d2a8896b1c2c7c1b9cccbc687:.env.example:generic-api-key:12
